## Why This Is Asked

Assesses CKNE-driven multi-tenant graph analytics design, focusing on per-tenant prioritization, adaptive traversal, caching, and safe degradation under load. Requires concrete parameters, fairness guarantees, observability hooks, and a test plan.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-routing and depth mapping
- Backpressure and resource reallocation
- Incremental caching and warm/cold budgets
- Telemetry, SLAs, and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
function adjustForCKNE(tenantCKNE, req) {
  const baseDepth = 6;
  if (tenantCKNE < 0.3) {
    req.depth = Math.min(req.depth || baseDepth, 3);
    req.rateLimit = Math.min(req.rateLimit || 200, 100);
  } else if (tenantCKNE > 0.7) {
    req.depth = Math.max(req.depth || baseDepth, 8);
    req.cacheHint = 'warm';
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation loop under bursty CKNE distributions?
- How would you ensure fairness when CKNE changes dynamically?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to design a CKNE-aware inference gateway that dynamically allocates shared compute resources across tenants with real-time rebalancing, ensuring SLA adherence and fairness under burst traffic conditions.

## Key Concepts

- **CKNE-to-quotas mapping**: Per-tenant concurrency limits, token budgets, and model selection strategies
- **Real-time degradation loop**: Minute-by-minute capacity reallocation and preemption mechanisms
- **Telemetry and fairness**: SLA monitoring, starvation prevention, resource accounting, and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: compute per-tenant max concurrent inferences from CKNE
function quotaForCKNE(ckne, base = 1, max = 8) {
  return Math.floor(base + (max - base) * ckne);
}

// Pseudo: minute-by-minute degradation loop
async function degradationLoop() {
  while (true) {
    const metrics = await gatherTelemetry();
    const overloaded = metrics.utilization > 0.8;

    if (overloaded) {
      await reallocateCapacity(metrics);
    }

    await sleep(60000); // 1 minute
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Edge gateways must enforce multi-tenant QoS using dynamic health signals. This tests a practical, beginner-friendly CKNE rate-limiting pattern that combines health signals with per-tenant control.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-capacity mapping and fairness
- Per-tenant token bucket implementation
- Minute-by-minute degradation loop with cooldowns
- Minimal payloads and testability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal token bucket per tenant
class TB {
  constructor(baseRate){ this.baseRate=baseRate; this.ckne=1; this.tokens=baseRate; this.last=Date.now(); }
  refill(now){
    const dt=(now-this.last)/1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.tokens + dt * this.baseRate * this.ckne, this.baseRate);
    this.last = now;
  }
  tryConsume(n=1){
    this.refill(Date.now());
    if(this.tokens >= n){ this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

